{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Day</b> — There are conclusive proofs from the science of calculation that the arrangement of the seven [days] changes in all its parts, and never will a configuration of arrangement be found like another, even if the world stood thousands of tens of thousands of years. This is also explicit in Sefer Yetzirah. And on account of this, what is renewed on this day informs us about the coming day, one to another, and generation to generation, for in every day there is a new thing. And there is no objection from the words of Solomon: 'there is nothing new,' for he speaks about the general whole." "<b>Utters</b> — That it not be understood as ceasing, like מבוע, or like 'tells'; here: יביעון בפיהם, like: אחוך שמע לי." "And the meaning of: day and night — the time stands."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:27:49.728909667Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}